{"name":"Acropolis","hex":"#f8e2ca","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Acropolis","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The Athenian Acropolis.","↑ “Acropolis” listed in the Oxford English Dictionary, second edition (1989)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Acropolis"}],"entries":[{"title":"Acropolis","blocks":[{"text":"An acropolis was the settlement of an upper part of an ancient Greek city, especially a citadel, and frequently a hill with precipitous sides, mainly chosen for purposes of defense. The term is typically used to refer to the Acropolis of Athens, yet nearly every Greek city had an acropolis of its own. The term derives from the homonymous Greek word \"Ακρόπολις\", composed from \"akron\" (ἄκρον), which means \"top\", and \"polis\" (πόλις), which means \"city\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/The_Acropolis_of_Athens_on_June_1%2C_2021.jpg/330px-The_Acropolis_of_Athens_on_June_1%2C_2021.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acropolis","wikidata":"Q131668"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Lindos_Rhodes_3.jpg/500px-Lindos_Rhodes_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Lindos_Rhodes_3.jpg/1280px-Lindos_Rhodes_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Lindos_Rhodes_3.jpg/500px-Lindos_Rhodes_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Acropolis of Lindos, on the island of Rhodes, Greece","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALindos_Rhodes_3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/The_Parthenon_in_Athens.jpg/500px-The_Parthenon_in_Athens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaig